{
  "concept": "A reaction's energy diagram encodes two independent quantities: the activation barrier ΔG‡, which controls how fast the reaction goes, and ΔG°, the reactant-to-product energy difference, which controls whether the reaction is favorable.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "2x2 grid of four reaction energy diagrams, panel labels (a), (b), (c), (d) at upper-left of each panel",
    "Per panel: tall vertical gradient arrow on the left, cream at bottom to orange at top, with vertical text label 'Energy'",
    "Per panel: pale blue rectangular plotting field with thin gray left and bottom axis lines, no tick numbers",
    "Per panel: horizontal axis caption 'Reaction progress' below the field, followed by a magenta right-pointing arrow",
    "Per panel: smooth magenta energy curve — flat reactant plateau, single rounded transition-state maximum, flat product plateau",
    "Per panel: horizontal black dashed guide lines at the reactant level, at the transition-state maximum, and at the product level",
    "Per panel: double-headed vertical arrow between reactant dashed line and maximum dashed line labeled 'ΔG‡'",
    "Per panel: double-headed vertical arrow between reactant dashed line and product dashed line labeled 'ΔG°'",
    "Panel (a): low barrier, product far below reactants (fast, exergonic)",
    "Panel (b): tall barrier, product below reactants but by less (slow, exergonic)",
    "Panel (c): very low shallow hump, product slightly above reactants (fast, endergonic)",
    "Panel (d): very tall barrier, product clearly above reactants (slow, endergonic)",
    "Enlarged single-panel view used when one profile is selected, with ΔG‡ and ΔG° arrows highlighted in contrasting colors",
    "Readout badges in the enlarged view: 'Rate: fast / slow' tied to ΔG‡ and 'Thermodynamics: favorable (exergonic) / unfavorable (endergonic)' tied to ΔG°",
    "Transition-state marker dot at the curve apex with label '‡ transition state'"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "The four textbook panels are four samples from one continuous family of energy profiles; zooming to a single panel lets the two quantities be read precisely.",
      "label": "View",
      "options": ["Four-panel grid", "Single enlarged panel"],
      "default": "Four-panel grid"
    },
    {
      "id": "profileSelect",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "Fast/slow is set by the barrier height, while exergonic/endergonic is set by where the product level sits relative to the reactants — the four combinations are all possible.",
      "label": "Reaction profile",
      "options": ["(a) Fast, exergonic", "(b) Slow, exergonic", "(c) Fast, endergonic", "(d) Slow, endergonic", "Custom"],
      "default": "(a) Fast, exergonic"
    },
    {
      "id": "barrierHeight",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "Raising ΔG‡ makes the hump taller and the reaction slower without moving the product level at all — rate is independent of thermodynamics.",
      "label": "Activation barrier ΔG‡ (kJ/mol)",
      "range": [10, 120, 2],
      "default": 30
    },
    {
      "id": "productEnergy",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "Moving the product plateau up or down changes ΔG° and flips the reaction between exergonic and endergonic while the barrier can stay the same.",
      "label": "Product energy relative to reactants ΔG° (kJ/mol)",
      "range": [-80, 40, 2],
      "default": -50
    },
    {
      "id": "peakHover",
      "type": "hover",
      "teaches": "Each dashed level and arrow has a specific meaning: reactant level, transition state, product level, ΔG‡ and ΔG°.",
      "teaches_note": "labels appear only on demand so the enlarged panel stays uncluttered"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Four reactions, four profiles",
      "narration": "All four panels use the same axes and the same curve shape, so the only things that differ are the height of the hump and the level where the products land. Every reaction has its own energy profile.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Four-panel grid", "profileSelect": "(a) Fast, exergonic" }
    },
    {
      "title": "(a) Low barrier, downhill",
      "narration": "Zooming into panel (a): a small ΔG‡ means molecules clear the barrier easily, so the reaction is fast, and the product level sits well below the reactants, so it is strongly exergonic.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Single enlarged panel", "profileSelect": "(a) Fast, exergonic", "barrierHeight": 30, "productEnergy": -60 }
    },
    {
      "title": "(b) Tall barrier, still downhill",
      "narration": "Panel (b) is also exergonic, yet the barrier is much taller. A favorable ΔG° does not guarantee a fast reaction — this one crawls.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Single enlarged panel", "profileSelect": "(b) Slow, exergonic", "barrierHeight": 100, "productEnergy": -30 }
    },
    {
      "title": "(c) Low barrier, slightly uphill",
      "narration": "Panel (c) has only a shallow hump, so the reaction is fast, but the products end up a little above the reactants: fast and endergonic, favoring reactants at equilibrium.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Single enlarged panel", "profileSelect": "(c) Fast, endergonic", "barrierHeight": 18, "productEnergy": 8 }
    },
    {
      "title": "(d) Tall barrier, uphill",
      "narration": "Panel (d) is the worst of both: a very tall ΔG‡ makes it slow, and a positive ΔG° makes it unfavorable.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Single enlarged panel", "profileSelect": "(d) Slow, endergonic", "barrierHeight": 115, "productEnergy": 30 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Dial them independently",
      "narration": "Now drag the two sliders. Hold ΔG° fixed and change the barrier: only the speed verdict changes. Hold the barrier fixed and drag the product level through zero: only the favorability verdict flips.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Single enlarged panel", "profileSelect": "Custom", "barrierHeight": 55, "productEnergy": -20 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "ΔG‡ and ΔG° are two separate knobs: barrier height decides how fast a reaction is, product-reactant energy difference decides whether it is favorable — a reaction can be any combination of fast/slow and favorable/unfavorable.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Recreate a clean 2x2 grid on white. Each cell: at its left a tall tapered arrow filled with a bottom-to-top gradient from pale cream to warm orange, pointing up, carrying rotated bold 'Energy' text; to its right a pale sky-blue filled rectangle acting as the plot field with thin gray left/bottom borders and no numeric ticks; beneath, centered caption 'Reaction progress' in serif-ish body type followed by a short magenta arrow pointing right. Inside each field draw a thick magenta (deep pink) sigmoid-over-hump curve: flat reactant plateau on the left, one smooth rounded maximum, smooth descent to a flat product plateau on the right, plateaus drawn with short horizontal dashed black extensions. Add black dashed horizontal guides at reactant, apex, and product levels, plus thin double-headed vertical arrows: one between reactant and apex guides labeled italic 'ΔG‡' and one between reactant and product guides labeled italic 'ΔG°'. Panel letters (a)-(d) bold in the top-left corner outside the blue field. Relative shapes: (a) low hump, deep drop; (b) very tall hump, moderate drop; (c) shallow hump, product just above reactants; (d) tallest hump, product clearly above reactants. In the enlarged single-panel mode use the same styling at larger scale, tint the ΔG‡ arrow one accent color (e.g. orange) and the ΔG° arrow another (e.g. teal/blue), mark the apex with a small dot labeled '‡', and place two small text badges reporting the rate verdict (from barrier height) and the thermodynamic verdict (sign of ΔG°), with the product plateau redrawn live from the sliders and the curve remaining smooth for all slider settings including ΔG° above zero."
}

